label summons
[signal arg="bg:dorm_room_night"]
The note was waiting on his bunk before he'd even changed out of his forest clothes, still smelling of pine and someone else's blood.
Headmaster's office. Tonight. Don't make me send someone to fetch you.
Lan (Sus): (One condition. You'll answer some questions about your family. Honestly.)
Lan (Sus): (I've had a year to prepare an answer and the honest one is still I don't know.)
He left the montante where it was. Whatever this conversation needed, he didn't think it needed a sword.
label office
[signal arg="bg:headmaster_office"]
Berthram's office was smaller than Lan expected and considerably messier.
Paper stacked at angles, a sword on the wall that had never been cleaned for display, and a window standing wide open despite the evening chill.
Berthram sat with his boots on the desk and an apple in one hand, and none of the cheerful haze he wore in public. His eyes were entirely clear.
Berthram: Sit.
Berthram: You want to tell me why a rank {rank} commoner spent this afternoon cutting through six trained killers to protect a crown princess like he'd done it a hundred times before?
Lan (Acting): Would "I panicked and it went well" be an acceptable answer?
Berthram: It would be a creative answer. It would also be a lie, and I'd know the second it left your mouth.
Berthram: I watched you in a forest a year ago hold off a beast that should have killed you twice over.
Berthram: I know what raw talent looks like, and I know what trained looks like,
Berthram: and what you did in that clearing was the second one, dressed up carefully to look like the first.
Berthram: You've been hiding your strength since you walked through my gate.
Berthram: I let it go because a boy's reasons for laying low are his own business.
Berthram: Today a princess of Britannic Arcana has watched you fight for real, and her questions are no longer just her business.
Berthram: They're mine.
Berthram: So. Start with the part I asked for a year ago. Your father. Tell me about him.
Lan (Default): Narr Schwert. Farmer, mostly. Woodcutter.
Lan (Default): Raised me in a valley in the Ritterland highlands until I was ten,
Lan (Default): then sent me out to make my own way, same as the rest of the family does with every child.
Berthram: Every child.
Lan (Default): It's tradition. Nobody teaches you gi, shir or the sword until you turn ten.
Lan (Default): Then you're exiled with a wooden sword and told not to come back until you've made a name for yourself.
Lan (Default): I don't know why. I asked. Nobody would answer.
Berthram: Before you were born. Do you know what he did, where he went, who he might have been?
Lan (Sus): No. He's never talked about it.
Lan (Sus): There was a trader once, when I was seven, who saw him chopping wood, went white as a sheet and turned his cart around.
Lan (Sus): I asked at dinner. My father said the man would've known him back when people called him "the Fool."
Lan (Sus): My mother didn't smile when he said it. She smiles at everything. That's the only reason I remember the conversation.
The silence went on long enough that Lan started counting his own heartbeats.
Berthram: The Fool.
Lan (Default): That mean something to you?
Berthram: It means I spent the better part of my thirties hunting a masked lunatic who humiliated half the corrupt nobility on this continent with nothing but a stick and a gift for making grown men weep in front of their own soldiers.
Berthram: I never once laid a hand on him. He disappeared near enough thirty years ago.
Berthram: Your father's name is Schwert. A trader who recognized him went the color of old paper.
Berthram: And you, a boy raised with no formal training who moves like he's been drilled by a master since birth, walk through my gate.
Lan (Sus): You think my father is the Fool.
Berthram: I think I don't know, and not knowing will keep me up at night for a while yet.
Berthram: I also think, watching your face, that you genuinely didn't know either.
Lan (Smile): That's actually a relief. I've spent a year assuming I was the crazy one for thinking something didn't add up.
Berthram: You weren't crazy. You were correct, and outmatched by people considerably better at keeping secrets than you are at finding them.
label truth
Berthram: One more thing, and this is the only question I'll press. Why the act? Why hide it from the day you arrived?
- "I don't want to be recruited."
	Lan (Default): I don't want to be recruited. Into anything.
- "Peace and quiet, mostly."
	Lan (Smug): Peace and quiet, mostly. I hear recruiters have terrible timing.
	Berthram: Hm. Go on.
Lan (Default): The moment people see what I can actually do, I stop being a student and start being an asset.
Lan (Default): Assets get pointed at things whether they want to be or not.
Lan (Default): I'd rather be underestimated and free than impressive and owned.
Berthram: That is the single most reasonable thing anyone under thirty has ever said in this office.
Berthram: It's also going to fail completely, starting today, but I respect the instinct.
label arrangement
Berthram: Here is what I'm prepared to do. I won't announce what I was told today.
Berthram: I won't strip your rank or drag you in front of the faculty.
Berthram: I can't do anything about the princess's memory, but I can keep the rest of this school from finding out the way she did.
Lan (Default): And in exchange.
Berthram: You stop wasting my time pretending to be exactly as unremarkable as your file says. Not publicly.
Berthram: Keep whatever act you need for the rest of the school. But you and I are done playing that game in private.
Berthram: If something like today happens again, you come to me before it happens if you can, and after if you can't.
Berthram: And before the year's out, you sit down with me properly and we talk about your family,
Berthram: once you've thought about what you actually want to ask your father next time you go home.
Lan (Smile): That's fair.
Berthram: I know it is. I'm very good at my job.
Berthram: One more thing.
Berthram: Whatever you were venting out there today,
Berthram: and don't think I didn't notice you were enjoying yourself somewhere around the third one, I'd like to see it again.
Berthram: In a context that doesn't involve assassins.
Berthram: You've got a teacher in this building who knows what to do with a blade like that. It isn't Nazr, and it isn't me.
Berthram: Go home. Sleep.
Berthram: For what it's worth, if your father really is who I think he is,
Berthram: he did more good under that mask than most knights manage in a lifetime under their own names.
Berthram: Whatever your family's hiding from you, I don't think it's shame.
Lan let himself out into the cold and walked back across the dark quad with a great deal more to think about than he'd had that morning.
